Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Film Courage: How does a writer create conflict?

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.380
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher&nbsp;
and Consultant: Conflict is created by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:10.440
opposing forces. If we're talking about the main&nbsp;
conflict in a story, that's the protagonist trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.940
to pursue their goal and then whatever main&nbsp;
force of opposition is getting in their way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.900
It can manifest in a lot of different ways but&nbsp;
it's whatever that main thing is that's stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.980
the protagonist or not allowing them to easily and&nbsp;
quickly achieve that goal and it seems like let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.060
say with Hustle movie [Netflix] which we've talked&nbsp;
about earlier the Adam Sandler it's a pretty clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.540
idea of what that conflict is. But if you look at&nbsp;
let's say Forrest Gump [1994] an older version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:46.320
a movie it's not actually that clear there's many&nbsp;
things he's encountering and that's another reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.680
that I would call Forrest Gump an advanced craft&nbsp;
story right because it's pretty non-traditional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.500
It's you have one guy and I don't know that I&nbsp;
would say he has a very clear end point that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:05.340
working toward it's a little bit episodic we're&nbsp;
watching him go through history different notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.440
points in history and accidentally showing up&nbsp;
in the right place at the wrong time or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.100
so that is a that's one of those stories that I&nbsp;
think would be a difficult one for a newer writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:24.780
to try to make work because if you don't have that&nbsp;
like standard framework of character pursuing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:31.320
goal to give you that spine and that main conflict&nbsp;
to rely on then you have to use other tools in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.960
your toolkit to create enough conflict and enough&nbsp;
engagement to keep us invested right to keep us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.740
engaged in the story and not checking out and&nbsp;
turning it off so I think Forrest Gump does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.860
through like just the novelty of the character&nbsp;
right like he's what's that guy going to do next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.320
like where is he going to show up and what what&nbsp;
famous moment in history are we going to show it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.900
see him pop up in so I think that's part of the&nbsp;
appeal of that story and then also within within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.460
each little segment of story I do think that&nbsp;
there is conflict it's not like I'm saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

